 and the most common  methods of animal identification  or permanent pen marks ear notches in  metal ear tags  research has shown these methods of  identification are prone to miss reading  eros at rates between 20 and 40 percent  in addition to animal models the NIH  recently reported significant levels of  miss on identification with cell lines  the lack of reliable identification of  inputs to your research is further  compounded by the lack of data quality  control rising from the use of paper lab  notebooks and spreadsheets manual  recording of experimental data manual  input of results data they leave the  process open to human error  transcription issues and do not provide  audit trails and lack transparency so  the lack of confidence in your data  quality arising from your analog methods  of identification poor data management  and limited rigor applied to the process  of experimental design execution and  analysis is really creating a house of  cards the wrong Mouse the wrong weight  the wrong tumor measurement all leading  to the wrong conclusions which is  certainly evident in the invivo  preclinical research the missing links  are at the heart of the reproductive  ability crisis we see an in vivo  research so how do we ensure smart lab  is alcoa compliant and don't forget CC  EA to complete consistent enduring and  available at so mark we have worked with  over 300 customers over the past six  years to better understand what makes a  lab smart and how to deliver that  capability in an affordable way we  believe the answer or the missing link  in the lab that prevents us from being  smart efficient accurate transparent  rigorous reproducible is digital  identity  Asian digital identification is the  technology that is connected seamlessly  to automated data collection processing  and analytical processes at the heart of  the so mark smart lab is a common method  of digital identification we propose the  adoption of the rain' rfid standard for  identifying all your lab objects  including your staff your animals your  samples and your devices passive  ultra-high frequency tags are already  used in billions of devices and  extensively in logistics to track assets  in real time and space it's a proven  low-cost accessible and scalable  technology platform already at the heart  of industry 4.0 and will become part of  the standard in the lab 4.0 revolution 2  at so mark we've developed the world's  first ultra high frequency animal tag  this provides preclinical in vivo  research a digital method of  identification that is low-cost more  humane for the animal and most  importantly is interoperable with other  brain RFID tags that can be used with  caged cards samples and lab instrument  assets tracking so you may be using  current available RFID technology in  your labs for animal identification but  these are not the same ultra-high  frequency or rain' compliant for example  they would not integrate with a sample  or a personnel identification badges  additionally the current RFID tags are  not purposely built or designed  specifically for animals which is an  animal welfare concern so now you've  solved the missing link in your lab and  all your objects are digital you have to  be able to connect those objects to your  research processes the right people the  right example experiments the right data  records to solve that challenge we  developed since ALAB which is a  technology platform providing tag  connectivity to lab devices and animals  and other inputs to a cloud-based data  system that correctly stores the records  and maintains full audit trails since  the lab is built to deliver automated  processes  across the entire chain of custody all  you need is to tag your objects plug in  your readers and devices and connect to  the internet since the lab enables you  to move away from paper and Excel sheets  to secure scalable cloud environment and  will connect with the technology you  already have your existing devices and  systems if you're lucky enough to have  good data systems so what does this look  like in practice utilizing our low-cost  benchtop tag reader or you could buy a  handheld reader our device enables you  to turn your existing lab instruments  into Internet of Things devices by  connecting them to our I connect system  the I connect captures the ID of the  person the animal the object and the  data from the lab device and  automatically puts the data in the right  record with an accurate time and date  stamp in the cloud database one of the  key steps towards implementing the smart  lab after adding digital IDs to your  objects is to use systems like I connect  to then automate the collection of data  from experiments and post them to the  right records in the cloud database with  a full audit trail that's what I connect  us in our smart lab we don't simply  upload a spreadsheet into an electronic  lab notebook we use since a lab to  ensure every data record is tracked  inversion independently with a full  audit trail recording where it came from  who recorded it when and from which  digitally identified subject for example  a mouse or what other inputs are  associated such as which cell line our  compound remember simply uploading files  to an electronic lab notebooks does not  give you full control over the source  data and the files and one of the  benefits of moving to a smart lab is not  just a mutable data it's actually much  more efficient we recently conducted 12  pilot studies across US and Europe  results were 69  74 percent reduction in time to take  data with a zero percent read or data  input errors not only did we achieve  process efficiency now you have a smart  lab with efficiently managed digital  data and now you may ask so what  well the real value comes from the  analysis of the data in the late 80s or  early 90s there was an often quoted  saying in the early days of data science  and I'm sure you will recognize it and  that was garbage N equals garbage out  there's significant excitement and  rightfully so about machine learning and  AI  but if you're training algorithms on  poor data quality really how good are  your algorithms I have provided two  reference articles in the presentation  that focus specifically on this crisis  and science the key strategic advantage  of a smart lab based on digital ID and  automated data collection is the data  authenticity accuracy and reliability  and what you will see is the higher  quality of data will drive insights  faster you can even start to move  towards near real-time discovery of  exceptions of errors of trends IBBs  a laboratory information technology  company estimated that saving one week  in drug discovery process is worth six  million dollars one of the advantages of  this move to digital data is you move  away from using Excel and manual  manipulation of data preparation and  transfer the minute you download data  from your repository again you open up a  [ __ ] in the chain of custody so the  final missing link that stops the chain  of custody from being broken is to keep  your research data in a secure data  environment and actually to point your  analysis tools at it preserving the  integrity of the data we propose using  data visualization tools you can gain  insights into your data quicker tools  such as power bi or tableau enable you  to set alerts when they detect anomalies  and data preventing your research going  out of control losing animals to ill  health unnecessarily and will save you  time and sometimes the value of your  research  if your data is locked in paper  notebooks or files on servers are even  files uploaded to an electronic lab  notebook you will struggle to develop  reliable analytical insights and of  course when you start to apply  predictive analytic tools machine  learning and AI to augment the scope of  your analysis and identify hidden  correlations you did not expect basing  these discoveries unreliable accurate  